    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d811">

                <pb facs="0892" n="788" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg788"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00 Woodward Mission/15: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Beaulac</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-01-06T12:00:00-05:45">January 6,
                            1932—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 2:02 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2. President Moncada yesterday expressed
                    to me personally his great pleasure at Admiral
                        Woodward’s designation as head of the American electoral mission
                    to supervise this year’s presidential elections in Nicaragua. He said he would
                    do everything within his power to cooperate with Admiral Woodward to insure the success of his mission. The
                    Nicaraguan press and people have likewise commented very favorably on Admiral Woodward’s designation. They regard
                    it as a new and hopeful step toward the establishment of permanent stable
                    government in Nicaragua.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beaulac</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
